<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-vector-geom-norm-in-R2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Norm in <m> \R^2 </m></title>

<introduction><p>
	In each of the following:
	<ol>

		<li> Draw <m> \uvec{v} </m> in the <m> xy </m>-plane with its initial point at the origin. </li>

		<li>
			On the same diagram,
			draw in a geometric representation of the decomposition of <m> \uvec{v} </m> as a linear combination of the standard basis vectors
			(<xref ref="subsection-vectors-concepts-standard-basis" />).
		</li>

		<li>
			Compute the norm of <m> \uvec{v} </m>,
			and relate the computation to the geometry of your diagram,
			via Pythagoras.
		</li>

	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> \uvec{v} = (2,5) </m></p></statement>

	<answer>

		<p><m> \uvec{v} = 2 \uvec{e}_1 + 5 \uvec{e}_2 </m></p>

		<image width="26.7%">
			<latex-image label="image-vector-geom-exercises-norm-in-R2-quadrant-1">
				<xi:include href="norm-in-R2.d/quadrant-1.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating vector norm in two dimensions via Pythagoras. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of a right triangle in the first quadrant of the <m>xy</m>-plane,
				with a directed line segment for each side.
				One leg is a vector labelled <m> 2 \uvec{e}_1 </m>,
				extending rightward from the origin along the <m> x </m>-axis to the point <m> (2,0) </m>.
				The second leg is a vector labelled <m> 5 \uvec{e}_2 </m>,
				extending upward from the point <m> (2,0) </m> on the <m> x </m>-axis to the point <m> (2,5) </m>.
				The hypotenuse is a vector labelled <m> \uvec{v} </m>,
				extending from the origin to the point <m> (2,5) </m>.
			</p></description>
		</image>

		<p><m> \unorm{v} = \sqrt{2^2 + 5^2} </m></p>

		<p>
		This is the Pythagorean formula for the length of the hypotenuse of the right triangle in the diagram above.
		</p>

	</answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> \uvec{v} = (-3,2) </m></p></statement>

	<answer>

		<p><m> \uvec{v} = (-3) \uvec{e}_1 + 2 \uvec{e}_2 </m></p>

		<image width="36.1%">
			<latex-image label="image-vector-geom-exercises-norm-in-R2-quadrant-2">
				<xi:include href="norm-in-R2.d/quadrant-2.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating vector norm in two dimensions via Pythagoras. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of a right triangle in the second quadrant of the <m>xy</m>-plane,
				with a directed line segment for each side.
				One leg is a vector labelled <m> (-3) \uvec{e}_1 </m>,
				extending leftward from the origin along the <m> x </m>-axis to the point <m> (-3,0) </m>.
				The second leg is a vector labelled <m> 2 \uvec{e}_2 </m>,
				extending upward from the point <m> (-3,0) </m> on the <m> x </m>-axis to the point <m> (-3,2) </m>.
				The hypotenuse is a vector labelled <m> \uvec{v} </m>,
				extending from the origin to the point <m> (-3,2) </m>.
			</p></description>
		</image>

		<p><md>
			<mrow> \unorm{v} \amp = \sqrt{{(-3)}^2 + 2^2} </mrow>
			<mrow> \amp = \sqrt{3^2 + 2^2} </mrow>
		</md></p>

		<p>
		After simplifying away the negative on the <m> x </m>-component,
		this is the Pythagorean formula for the length of the hypotenuse of the right triangle in the diagram above.
		</p>

	</answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> \uvec{v} = (1,-4) </m></p></statement>

	<answer>

		<p><m> \uvec{v} = 1 \uvec{e}_1 + (-4) \uvec{e}_2 </m></p>

		<image width="22.9%">
			<latex-image label="image-vector-geom-exercises-norm-in-R2-quadrant-4">
				<xi:include href="norm-in-R2.d/quadrant-4.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating vector norm in two dimensions via Pythagoras. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of a right triangle in the fourth quadrant of the <m>xy</m>-plane,
				with a directed line segment for each side.
				One leg is a vector labelled <m> 1 \uvec{e}_1 </m>,
				extending rightward from the origin along the <m> x </m>-axis to the point <m> (1,0) </m>.
				The second leg is a vector labelled <m> (-4) \uvec{e}_2 </m>,
				extending downward from the point <m> (1,0) </m> on the <m> x </m>-axis to the point <m> (1,-4) </m>.
				The hypotenuse is a vector labelled <m> \uvec{v} </m>,
				extending from the origin to the point <m> (1,-4) </m>.
			</p></description>
		</image>

		<p><md>
			<mrow> \unorm{v} \amp = \sqrt{1^2 + {(-4)}^2} </mrow>
			<mrow> \amp = \sqrt{1^2 + 4^2} </mrow>
		</md></p>

		<p>
		After simplifying away the negative on the <m> y </m>-component,
		this is the Pythagorean formula for the length of the hypotenuse of the right triangle in the diagram above.
		</p>

	</answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> \uvec{v} = (-\pi,-3) </m></p></statement>

	<answer>

		<p><m> \uvec{v} = (-\pi) \uvec{e}_1 + (-3) \uvec{e}_2 </m></p>

		<image width="45.8%">
			<latex-image label="image-vector-geom-exercises-norm-in-R2-quadrant-3">
				<xi:include href="norm-in-R2.d/quadrant-3.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating vector norm in two dimensions via Pythagoras. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of a right triangle in the third quadrant of the <m>xy</m>-plane,
				with a directed line segment for each side.
				One leg is a vector labelled <m> (-pi) \uvec{e}_1 </m>,
				extending leftward from the origin along the <m> x </m>-axis to the point <m> (-\pi,0) </m>.
				The second leg is a vector labelled <m> (-3) \uvec{e}_2 </m>,
				extending downward from the point <m> (-\pi,0) </m> on the <m> x </m>-axis to the point <m> (-\pi,-3) </m>.
				The hypotenuse is a vector labelled <m> \uvec{v} </m>,
				extending from the origin to the point <m> (-\pi,-3) </m>.
			</p></description>
		</image>

		<p><md>
			<mrow> \unorm{v} \amp = \sqrt{(-\pi)^2 + {(-3)}^2} </mrow>
			<mrow> \amp = \sqrt{\pi^2 + 3^2} </mrow>
		</md></p>

		<p>
		After simplifying away the negatives on the components,
		this is the Pythagorean formula for the length of the hypotenuse of the right triangle in the diagram above.
		</p>

	</answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
